  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nSaturday - Sunday: Light Rain Chances Saturday, Drier Sunday\n\nAs we head into the afternoon on Saturday, a shortwave trough will \npush through the Great Plains and into the Upper Midwest with an \nincreasing southerly moisture fetch. In the presence of this wave, \n850mb moisture transport increasing fairly considerably with \nprecipitable water values increasing to around 1.5\" in the vicinity \nof the I-35 corridor. As a result, much of the CAMs generally agree \non shifting an axis of light precipitation with the shortwave \nthrough portions of northeast IA and southeast MN during the morning \nhours on Friday with high confidence (over 80%) for measurable \nprecipitation in the NBM for areas near I-35. However, as this axis \nof rain pushes eastward, it will be more removed from the \naforementioned 850mb moisture plume and thus have more dry air to \ncontend with. Consequently, much of the CAMs generally agree on \nweakening this area of precipitation as it moves east resulting in \nlower chances for precipitation once you get east of the Mississippi \nRiver. As far as rainfall amounts are concerned, the highest \nprobabilities (20-50%) for amounts over 0.25\" in the recent HREF \ngenerally remains along US-52 and west where the better moisture is \nlocated. In the wake of this shortwave, upper-level ridging builds \ninto the region for Sunday resulting in drier conditions for much of \nthe daytime hours. \n\nMonday - Friday: Dry Monday, Rain Potential on Tuesday\n\nThe aforementioned ridging remains in place for Monday resulting in \ncontinued dry conditions. With the ridging in place and very weak \nsoutherly surface flow, would expect some increase in temperatures \nfor Monday with median high temperatures in the NBM reaching into \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s areawide. However, as we head into \nTuesday a broader trough swings into the area from the Canadian \nRockies with an attending cold front. As this occurs, much of the \ngrand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) agrees on bringing \nmeasurable precipitation into the region with high probabilities (70-\n90%) during the Tuesday timeframe. One noticeable difference from \nyesterday is that probabilities for 0.25\" amounts or greater have \ndecreased (20-50%) in the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble). When looking at the cluster analysis of the grand \nensemble during this period, the general trend that is noted is that \nthe majority of members (roughly 75%) keep a secondary upper-level \ntrough over the Southern Rockies for a longer period which does not \nphase with this upstream wave. Consequently, thinking our Tuesday \nwave may be more progressive and cut off from the better\nmoisture associated with the secondary wave which would in turn\nlimit rainfall totals.\n\nThe downstream implication of this ensemble favored scenario is the \naforementioned Southern Rockies upper-level trough takes longer to \neject into the Great Plains during the week. As a result, questions \nlinger as to how long it would take before and if any precipitation \nwould reach the region. Consequently, the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian) generally keeps measurable precipitation modest \n(30-50%) through Friday due to this uncertainty. Regardless, still \nsomething to watch as this feature will likely have a respectable \namount of moisture with it as it interacts with the remnants of \nHurricane Polo, so certainly could see some healthy rainfall amounts \nwhere this system tracks. In any case, with relatively weak surface \nflow and minimal change to the synoptic stream flow aloft, thinking \nthat temperatures will remain relatively steady-state with highs \ngenerally in the 60s to lower 70s throughout the week, as supported \nin the 50th percentile NBM.",
